One, in reality, we can experiment with whatever&nbsp;
objects we have at our disposal, but in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:53.280
simulation, we can do anything. Including changing&nbsp;
the physical parameters of these objects, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:00.640
thus, this previous work can simulate three jello&nbsp;
blocks of different stiffness values. So cool!

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:08.400
Two, this technique simulates strong two-way&nbsp;
coupling. What does that mean? Well, you see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:15.680
simulation here that doesn’t have it. So is this&nbsp;
correct? Well, not quite. The honey should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:23.440
supporting the dipper, and it not only falls, but&nbsp;
it falls in a very unnatural way. Instead, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:31.280
technique can simulate strong two-way coupling,&nbsp;
and finally it gets this right. And not only that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:38.560
but what I really love about this is that it also&nbsp;
gets small nuances right. I will try to speed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.800
the footage a little, so you can see that the&nbsp;
honey doesn’t only support the dipper, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:53.360
dipper still has some subtle movements both&nbsp;
in reality and in the simulation. A+. Love it.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:02.560
Three, we can even simulate the physics of baking&nbsp;
on a computer. Let’s see…yes, expansion and baking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:10.800
is happening here. Are we done? Well, let’s have&nbsp;
a look inside. Yup, this is a good one. Yum!

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:19.280
And perhaps my favorite work in this area is&nbsp;
this. Four, now, you may be wondering, Károly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:26.720
why are you showing a real video to me? Well,&nbsp;
this is not a real video. This is a simulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:34.160
And so is this one. And hold on to your papers,&nbsp;
because this is not a screenshot taken from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:44.000
simulation, nuh-uh. No sir. This is a real photo.&nbsp;
How cool is that? Whenever you feel a little sad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.640
just think about the fact that through the&nbsp;
power of computer graphics research, we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:56.560
