a small slice of reality. Absolutely amazing.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:03.200
However, there is a problem. And the&nbsp;
problem is that all of these simulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:09.920
have a price. And that price is time.&nbsp;
Oh yes, some of these works require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.720
long all-nighters to compute.
And, unfortunately, as a research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:20.880
field matures, it gets more and more difficult to&nbsp;
say something new, and improve upon previous work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:28.320
So, can we expect no more meaningful speedups&nbsp;
at this point? Are we at a saturation point?

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.320
Well, just two years ago, we talked about&nbsp;
a technique that was able to simulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:42.800
100 million particles. My goodness, this was&nbsp;
absolutely amazing. And now, just a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:51.280
more papers down the line, we go 10x. Yes, that’s&nbsp;
right! Here is an even better one that is finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:04:00.400
able to simulate 10 times as many particles. Yes,&nbsp;
1 billion particles. Now, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.680
